Early on those mornings, when rain came, 
we set our pails beneath the roof's mouth
and watched water accumulate like the past.
We were younger then, and trusted mother:
someone in heaven, she said, had left the taps
running, then fallen drowsy into bed.
It was not a dream. I still see the passage,
lantern-yellow, distressed with constipation
whenever we all moved in its belly.
Even now my uncle is waking in the house
with the sun caught in his eyes; his liver swells
like a wet sponge inside the basin of his skin.
I can see him lying dead on his mother's chest
like a bucket left outside to gather rain.

